WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:01.300
Hi trainees!

2
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:09.631
In this demonstration I will check two lab machines, the way a Level 1 analyst does when an alert names a host.

3
00:00:09.631 --> 00:00:13.908
First a Windows workstation, then an Ubuntu Linux server.

4
00:00:13.908 --> 00:00:18.486
Watch the commands, and watch the analyst notes on the right,

5
00:00:18.486 --> 00:00:23.440
because Activity 2.1 asks you to collect exactly this information.

6
00:00:24.539 --> 00:00:29.826
Question one: what is this machine's network identity?

7
00:00:29.826 --> 00:00:31.882
I type ipconfig /all.

8
00:00:31.882 --> 00:00:36.777
The host name is W K S dash T R N dash zero seven.

9
00:00:36.777 --> 00:00:39.909
The IPv4 address is 10.10.20.57.

10
00:00:39.909 --> 00:00:45.489
It starts with ten, so it is a private, internal address.

11
00:00:45.489 --> 00:00:54.300
The default gateway, 10.10.20.1, is the router this computer uses to reach other networks.

12
00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:56.747
I write both in my notes.

13
00:00:57.847 --> 00:01:02.274
Question two: what is this computer listening for?

14
00:01:02.274 --> 00:01:08.560
netstat -ano shows connections, with the process ID in the last column.

15
00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:15.731
I pipe it to findstr LISTENING, so I only see open ports waiting for connections.

16
00:01:15.731 --> 00:01:21.662
Ports 135 and 445 are normal Windows services; 445 is file sharing.

17
00:01:21.662 --> 00:01:23.610
But look at port 3389.

18
00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:25.646
That is Remote Desktop.

19
00:01:25.646 --> 00:01:30.073
Is Remote Desktop supposed to be on a workstation?

20
00:01:30.073 --> 00:01:36.535
The PID column says process 1180 owns it, so let's find out what that is.

21
00:01:37.635 --> 00:01:45.497
tasklist with slash S V C shows the services inside a process, and the filter picks only PID 1180.

22
00:01:45.497 --> 00:01:50.550
It is svchost, hosting TermService, the Remote Desktop service.

23
00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:54.481
So Remote Desktop is enabled on this workstation.

24
00:01:54.481 --> 00:01:59.053
That is not automatically an attack, but it is a finding.

25
00:01:59.053 --> 00:02:04.347
I note it, and later I check it against the organization's policy.

26
00:02:05.447 --> 00:02:08.121
Question three: what is running?

27
00:02:08.121 --> 00:02:11.714
In PowerShell, Get-Process lists processes.

28
00:02:11.714 --> 00:02:15.140
I sort them by CPU and show the top five.

29
00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:19.401
MsMpEng is the Microsoft Defender antivirus engine.

30
00:02:19.401 --> 00:02:22.159
Good, the security tool is alive.

31
00:02:22.159 --> 00:02:26.587
Explorer, Edge and OneDrive are normal user programs.

32
00:02:26.587 --> 00:02:34.775
A strange name, or a program running from a temp or Downloads folder, would deserve a closer look.

33
00:02:35.876 --> 00:02:38.683
Question four: what system is this?

34
00:02:38.683 --> 00:02:46.544
systeminfo prints a lot, so I filter for three lines: the OS name, the version, and the boot time.

35
00:02:46.544 --> 00:02:51.758
Windows 11 Enterprise, build 22631, started at 7:42 this morning.

36
00:02:51.758 --> 00:03:00.019
The version matters, because later in the course you will match versions against known vulnerabilities.

37
00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:03.515
Question five: who is logged in?

38
00:03:03.515 --> 00:03:11.448
query user shows trainee07, on the console, which means someone sitting at the machine, logged on at 7:44.

39
00:03:11.448 --> 00:03:18.784
If an alert came from this PC at two in the morning, this is how you would check who was using it.

40
00:03:19.884 --> 00:03:24.831
Now the same five questions on the Ubuntu Linux lab VM.

41
00:03:24.831 --> 00:03:28.249
The Linux version of ipconfig is ip a.

42
00:03:28.249 --> 00:03:33.916
Ignore lo, that is the loopback, the machine talking to itself.

43
00:03:33.916 --> 00:03:41.652
The real network card is E N P zero S three, and its address is 10.10.20.80, slash 24.

44
00:03:42.752 --> 00:03:46.449
Linux shows the gateway in its routing table.

45
00:03:46.449 --> 00:03:51.543
ip route, piped to grep default, shows default via 10.10.20.1.

46
00:03:51.543 --> 00:03:58.855
That is the same router as the Windows workstation, so both machines are on the same LAN.

47
00:03:59.956 --> 00:04:04.905
Listening ports on Linux: ss -tunap, filtered for LISTEN.

48
00:04:04.905 --> 00:04:10.375
I can see ports 53, 22 and 80, but the process column is empty.

49
00:04:10.375 --> 00:04:16.627
Those services belong to root, and a normal user cannot see their names.

50
00:04:16.627 --> 00:04:23.140
This is a very common stumbling block in Activity 2.1, so remember the fix.

51
00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:30.725
I run it again with sudo, which means: run this as administrator.

52
00:04:30.725 --> 00:04:34.616
Now the last column names each process.

53
00:04:34.616 --> 00:04:39.205
Port 22 is sshd, the SSH remote login service.

54
00:04:39.205 --> 00:04:42.497
Port 80 is apache2, a web server.

55
00:04:42.497 --> 00:04:45.790
And 53 is the local DNS resolver.

56
00:04:45.790 --> 00:04:50.280
For a lab web server, all three are expected.

57
00:04:51.379 --> 00:04:52.898
What is running?

58
00:04:52.898 --> 00:04:55.460
ps aux lists every process.

59
00:04:55.460 --> 00:05:00.205
I sort it by CPU and keep the top lines with head.

60
00:05:00.205 --> 00:05:06.563
Apache runs as the user www-data, which is normal for a web server.

61
00:05:06.563 --> 00:05:08.272
sshd runs as root.

62
00:05:08.272 --> 00:05:14.155
And the bash shell at the bottom is my own session as trainee.

63
00:05:15.255 --> 00:05:24.484
System information: hostnamectl gives the host name, the operating system, Ubuntu 24.04, and the kernel version.

64
00:05:24.484 --> 00:05:30.087
On older systems, uname dash A gives the kernel version in one line.

65
00:05:31.187 --> 00:05:34.501
Finally, who is logged in, and who was?

66
00:05:34.501 --> 00:05:36.625
last shows recent logins.

67
00:05:36.625 --> 00:05:40.363
trainee is still logged in from 10.10.20.57.

68
00:05:40.363 --> 00:05:44.440
That is the Windows workstation we just checked.

69
00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:51.492
Connecting the dots from one host to another like this is exactly what analysts do.

70
00:05:52.591 --> 00:05:58.425
This afternoon you will not need VirtualBox or a virtual machine on your own computer.

71
00:05:58.425 --> 00:06:00.867
You practice in the CTM Virtual Lab.

72
00:06:00.867 --> 00:06:06.564
Open the lab link from your trainer and sign in with your full name and seat number.

73
00:06:06.564 --> 00:06:12.805
First try the mission Practice: Demo 1, which has the same workstation and VM as this video,

74
00:06:12.805 --> 00:06:14.840
so your screen can match mine.

75
00:06:15.939 --> 00:06:16.696
Your turn.

76
00:06:16.696 --> 00:06:20.025
In the CTM Virtual Lab, choose Activity 2.1.

77
00:06:20.025 --> 00:06:28.951
You get your own Windows workstation and your own Ubuntu VM, with values different from mine and from your classmates.

78
00:06:28.951 --> 00:06:36.063
Run these commands on both hosts and record the results in the worksheet in your Lab Notebook.

79
00:06:36.063 --> 00:06:44.989
Then answer the analysis question: which ports are listening, which service owns each one, and is anything unexpected?

80
00:06:44.989 --> 00:06:49.755
Remember to use sudo with ss, and pin your outputs as evidence.

